% datamunge  Octave interface to the C++ library datamunge (generated by Rosetta).
%
%   addpath (DIR) on the directory holding +datamunge and __datamunge__.oct.
%
%   Classes:
%     datamunge.Expr  A symbolic expression tree over +, -, *, /, ^ (power) and sin/cos/exp/log, supporting symbolic differentiation, basic algebraic simplification, and numeric evaluation. Value type wrapping a shared, immutable node tree -- copies are cheap and structural sharing across copies is always safe since nodes are never mutated after construction.
%     datamunge.Polynomial  Dense univariate polynomial over the reals, stored as coefficients in ascending degree order (coefficients()[i] is the coefficient of x^i). Always kept trimmed -- no trailing (highest-degree) zero coefficients, except for the zero polynomial itself, which is stored as the single coefficient {0.0}.
%     datamunge.PolyExtendedGcdResult  Result of the extended Euclidean algorithm on two polynomials: `gcd`, plus the Bezout coefficients `s`/`t` such that `s.multiply(a).add(t.multiply(b)) == gcd`.
%     datamunge.RealRootIntervals  Isolating intervals for every distinct real root of p: parallel lower/upper bound vectors where each (lower[i], upper[i]) contains exactly one root. Found by bisecting a starting interval (a Cauchy bound on root magnitude) driven by sturm_root_count().
%     datamunge.Complex  A complex number, used to report polynomial roots that are not real. Plain re/im fields (no arithmetic operators exposed) matching Polynomial's convention of a member `operator==` over a free one (SWIG's Python backend maps a free `operator==` to a single module-level `__eq__` that collides across every type that defines one).
%     datamunge.Cplx  C++ class datamunge::algebra::detail::Cplx.
%     datamunge.SquareFreeFactor  One factor of a square-free decomposition: `factor` (itself square-free, i.e. no repeated roots) appears with multiplicity `multiplicity` in the original polynomial.
%     datamunge.RealFactor  One irreducible-over-R factor of a real factorization: `factor` is either linear (x - r) for a real root r, or an irreducible real quadratic (x^2 + bx + c, for a complex-conjugate root pair), appearing with `multiplicity` in the original polynomial.
%     datamunge.RealFactorization  Factors p over the reals into irreducible linear/quadratic pieces plus the overall scale needed to reconstruct p exactly: p == leading_coefficient * prod(factors[i].factor ^ factors[i].multiplicity).
%     datamunge.MultivariatePolynomial  Sparse multivariate polynomial over the reals in a fixed number of variables (x0, x1, ...), stored internally as a map from exponent vector to coefficient. Exposed to bindings via parallel arrays (exponents()/coefficients()) rather than the internal map, matching this module's std::map-avoidance convention for SWIG-facing types.
%     datamunge.GfFactorization  Complete factorization over GF(p) from factor_gf_p().
%     datamunge.RationalFunction  A ratio of two polynomials (numerator / denominator), kept automatically reduced by their GCD -- e.g. (x^2-1)/(x-1) simplifies to x+1 -- with the denominator normalized to monic.
%     datamunge.ArbitraryFunction  The most general objective function: only supports evaluation. Optimizers that consume this type (e.g. SimulatedAnnealing) are derivative-free.
%     datamunge.DifferentiableFunction  An objective function that additionally supports exact gradient evaluation. Optimizers that consume this type (GradientDescent, Adam, LBFGS) use the full gradient at every step.
%     datamunge.SeparableFunction  An objective that decomposes as a sum over num_functions() independent terms (e.g. one term per training example). evaluate() defaults to summing every term, but remains overridable for a more efficient full-objective computation.
%     datamunge.DifferentiableSeparableFunction  A function that is both differentiable and separable -- the common case for machine learning loss functions (a sum of per-example losses). Optimizers that consume this type (SGD) can update coordinates from a single term's gradient without evaluating the full objective.
%     datamunge.ProximalFunction  Composite objective for proximal methods. gradient() is the gradient of the smooth component, and proximal() applies the non-smooth component's proximal operator.
%     datamunge.HessianFunction  C++ class datamunge::optim::HessianFunction.
%     datamunge.EqualityConstrainedFunction  Smooth equality-constrained objective for constrained optimizers.
%     datamunge.InequalityConstrainedFunction  C++ class datamunge::optim::InequalityConstrainedFunction.
%     datamunge.ResidualFunction  C++ class datamunge::optim::ResidualFunction.
%     datamunge.GibbsOptions  Settings for GibbsSampler.
%     datamunge.GibbsResult  Output of GibbsSampler::sample().
%     datamunge.GibbsSampler  Metropolis-within-Gibbs: updates one coordinate at a time, each via a scalar random-walk Metropolis step evaluated against the SAME joint log-density with every other coordinate held fixed -- mathematically equivalent to a Metropolis step targeting the exact full conditional p(x_i | x_-i), since the joint-density ratio with everything else held fixed IS the full-conditional-density ratio. This is the standard fallback used whenever a full conditional isn't conjugate/directly sampleable (what BUGS/JAGS call "Metropolis-within-Gibbs"), not exact Gibbs sampling via closed-form conditional samplers -- those are necessarily model-specific (they need to know each conditional's actual distributional family) and aren't something a generic engine driven only by a joint log-density can provide. Updating one coordinate at a time (rather than proposing the whole vector jointly, as RandomWalkMetropolis does) can mix substantially better when parameters live on very different scales or are only weakly correlated.
%     datamunge.HMCOptions  Settings for HMC.
%     datamunge.HMCResult  Output of HMC::sample().
%     datamunge.HMC  Hamiltonian Monte Carlo with a fixed leapfrog trajectory length and Nesterov dual-averaging step-size adaptation during warmup (Hoffman & Gelman 2014), using an identity mass matrix. Samples from the distribution proportional to exp(log_posterior) over unconstrained real parameters.
%     datamunge.ImportanceSamplingOptions  Settings for ImportanceSampling.
%     datamunge.ImportanceSamplingResult  Output of ImportanceSampling::sample().
%     datamunge.ImportanceSampling  Importance sampling against a multivariate Gaussian proposal N(mean, covariance) -- a natural choice when a Laplace approximation is available and reasonably close to the target's shape (e.g. bayes::MAP's mode paired with a numerically estimated Hessian there). Produces a weighted i.i.d. sample in one shot rather than a Markov chain -- no warmup, nothing to check for mixing -- but accuracy depends entirely on how well the Gaussian proposal covers the target's mass; effective_sample_size is the diagnostic to check before trusting the result.
%     datamunge.MAPOptions  C++ class datamunge::bayes::MAPOptions.
%     datamunge.MAP  Maximum a posteriori estimation: finds the mode of a log-posterior density via L-BFGS (datamunge::optim::LBFGS), analogous to Stan's `optimizing()`.
%     datamunge.NUTSOptions  Settings for NUTS.
%     datamunge.NUTSResult  Output of NUTS::sample().
%     datamunge.NUTS  The No-U-Turn Sampler (Hoffman & Gelman 2014, the efficient slice-sampling variant, Algorithm 3): Hamiltonian Monte Carlo with an automatically chosen trajectory length -- the leapfrog trajectory is grown by repeated doubling until it would start turning back on itself (a "U-turn"), removing the need to hand-tune a fixed number of leapfrog steps. Step size is still adapted via the same dual-averaging scheme as HMC. Uses an identity mass matrix.
%     datamunge.RWMOptions  Settings for RandomWalkMetropolis.
%     datamunge.RWMResult  Output of RandomWalkMetropolis::sample().
%     datamunge.RandomWalkMetropolis  Random-walk Metropolis-Hastings: the classic gradient-free MCMC baseline, proposing x' = x + step_size * N(0, I) and accepting with probability min(1, exp(log_posterior(x') - log_posterior(x))). Complements HMC/NUTS (which need a gradient via optim::DifferentiableFunction) for targets where only a log-density evaluation is available -- a black-box model, a discontinuous/non-differentiable posterior, or a director-subclassed optim::ArbitraryFunction in a language without access to this library's autodiff.
%     datamunge.Pixel  A pixel value in fully-expanded RGBA form, regardless of the owning Image's actual mode -- always 4 plain uint8 fields (rather than a variable-length per-mode vector) so this stays trivially bindable across languages, matching this codebase's established preference for fixed-shape structs. get_pixel()/set_pixel() handle the expansion/truncation to and from the Image's real stored channel count.
%     datamunge.Image  A 2D raster image: row-major, top-left origin, interleaved channel bytes (matching the layout every common image codec uses on disk, so I/O needs no reshuffling). Pixel data is always stored as the mode's exact channel count (1/2/3/4 bytes per pixel) -- get_pixel()/set_pixel() are the only place mode-specific expansion happens.
%     datamunge.HSV  A pixel color in HSV space: h in [0, 360), s and v in [0, 1].
%     datamunge.KeyPoint  A detected blob keypoint: its pixel location, the Gaussian scale (sigma) at which it was found, and the Difference-of-Gaussians response at that scale (higher magnitude is a stronger blob).
%     datamunge.Corner  A detected corner: its pixel location and a detector-specific response score (higher is a stronger corner). Meant to be independently sortable/filterable by the caller.
%     datamunge.StructureTensorField  C++ class datamunge::cv::detail::StructureTensorField.
%     datamunge.HoughLine  A detected line in the classical (rho, theta) normal-form parameterization: every point (x, y) on the line satisfies x*cos(theta) + y*sin(theta) = rho. Represents an INFINITE line, not a segment -- see line_endpoints() to clip it to an image.
%     datamunge.HoughCircle  A detected circle: center, radius, and the raw vote count that found it.
%     datamunge.FlowVector  The estimated motion of one tracked point between two frames. `valid` is false when the point's window was too close to the image border, or too textureless/aperture- ambiguous (the same "does the structure tensor have two strong eigenvalues" criterion shi_tomasi_corners() uses to pick good points to track in the first place -- Lucas-Kanade windows should generally be centered on such points).
%     datamunge.LabelMap  The result of connected_components(): a per-pixel label image (row-major, same layout as image::Image::data() but one int per pixel rather than per channel byte). Label 0 is always background; foreground components are numbered 1..count.
%     datamunge.ImageBatch  One batch of images and labels returned by ImageLoader.
%     datamunge.ImageLoader  A configurable, disk-backed batch iterator over a standard image dataset.
%     datamunge.Mesh1D  A 1D finite-element mesh: a strictly increasing sequence of node coordinates. Element i spans [nodes[i], nodes[i+1]] for i in [0, num_elements()) -- no separate connectivity array is needed since consecutive nodes are always the two endpoints of one linear (P1) element. `nodes` is a public field for direct C++ construction, but (matching datamunge::ode::ODESolution's t/y fields -- a swig-jse R-backend codegen bug hits plain vector<double> struct *field* getters) is not exposed to language bindings; node_at()/num_nodes() are the cross-language-safe accessors.
%     datamunge.ScalarField1D  A user-supplied scalar source-term function of one spatial variable, f(x). Director-enabled extension point (same pattern as datamunge::ode::RHS and datamunge::optim::ArbitraryFunction elsewhere in this codebase): subclass and override evaluate() in C++, or, via SWIG directors, in any binding with director support. Bindings without director support must instead use FEM1D::solve_builtin()'s fixed set of named source terms.
%     datamunge.ScalarField2D  A user-supplied scalar source-term function of two spatial variables, f(x, y). Same director-enabled pattern as ScalarField1D; FEM2D::solve_builtin() is the fallback for bindings without director support.
%     datamunge.BoundaryCondition1D  Owned endpoint data for Dirichlet, outward-conormal Neumann or Robin conditions. Dirichlet uses value as u; Neumann uses it as p*du/dn; Robin uses it as the reference value in p*du/dn+alpha*(u-value)=0. Alpha zero gives zero conormal. For coercive diffusion/heat models use nonnegative Robin alpha and sufficient constraints/reaction to remove the constant nullspace. Unsupported enum values and nonfinite data are outside the solver's preconditions, not fully validated.
%     datamunge.FEM1DResult  Owned nodal P1 steady solution, with checked scalar accessors for language bindings. The continuous finite-element field is the piecewise-linear interpolant of these values; nodal agreement alone does not imply agreement between nodes. Copies own their arrays, with no mesh/callback alias. Public edits can break array consistency; accessors check the actual requested vector bounds.
%     datamunge.FEM1DTimeSeries  Owned initial state and actual strictly advancing backward-Euler time slices. Solver output has one u row per time and one value per spatial node. Initial time zero/u0 are retained as supplied; later rows satisfy imposed boundary data. Times retain rounded nominal-step products with a shortened final interval; an already reached endpoint is not appended twice. Copies retain no mesh/source alias. Public edits may break consistency; accessors check actual vector bounds.
%     datamunge.FEM1D  Solve steady or transient one-dimensional divergence-form problems with P1 Galerkin FEM. Models -(p*u')'+q*u=f and u_t-(p*u')'+q*u=f on a caller-supplied mesh. Requires at least two finite strictly increasing nodes, finite coefficient/ boundary/source data and finite assembled arithmetic with nonzero Thomas pivots; those mathematical/geometry preconditions are not fully validated. Positive p, nonnegative q/Robin data and adequate constraints give the usual coercive model. Piecewise-constant p/q can be scalar or one per element. Results own their values; input mesh/data are unchanged. Calls share no mutable global state, while callback side effects/concurrency belong to the caller.
%     datamunge.Point2D  A copied point or free vector with ordinary double coordinates. Construction does not validate finiteness; checked algorithms document their own input contracts. Default coordinates are zero. Equality is numeric, so signed zeros compare equal and NaN coordinates do not compare equal.
%     datamunge.Triangle  Three original point indices in counterclockwise order for finite mesh faces. Original field layout/defaults are retained. A trace ghost uses c=points.size() as a symbolic infinity marker, with a,b the reversed finite hull edge; it is never returned by delaunay_triangulation(). No coordinate is constructed for it.
%     datamunge.DelaunayWork  Actual seed and cavity operations, excluding validation, sorting and copies. A conflict test is either an incircle test on a finite face or an exact half-plane test on a ghost. Counters exclude CCW checks on newly created faces, between-endpoint comparisons, map operations, allocation, trace copies and final sorting. Initial seed plus three ghosts are excluded from creation/removal counts. No counts are estimates of time or fixed-predicate internal arithmetic.
%     datamunge.DelaunayInsertion  Owned actual cavity removal, directed boundary and replacement for one site. removed/added may contain ghosts, identified by c=the original input count. boundary is ordered by canonical undirected endpoint key but retains the first directed occurrence; shared interior edges are cancelled. Actual face scans precede this event. No full before/after mesh snapshot or geometric replay is retained. All vectors own their entries and have no input lifetime dependency.
%     datamunge.DelaunayTrace  Complete owned sites, earliest-identity mapping, finite mesh and actual events. Numeric coordinate equality coalesces duplicates and signed zeros. Representatives retain earliest input indices in input order. Seed is the first two distinct sites and first later noncollinear site; remaining distinct sites are inserted in input order. Strict positive incircle conflicts retain an existing diagonal on an exact cocircular tie; permutations may therefore select another valid mesh. Final finite triangles rotate their smallest id first and are lexicographically sorted, independent of map/hash traversal. All-collinear/fewer than three distinct sites have no seed, faces or insertion events. All fields own data; public edits are unchecked and independent calls share no mutable state.
%     datamunge.DelaunayAnalysis  Coordinate-deduplicated topology counts and exact local empty-circle checks. Counts are independent of triangle order. Duplicate-coordinate ids are mapped to earliest originals. Triangle indices must be valid, distinct in coordinate, exactly CCW, with no duplicate face and at most two incident faces per edge. These checks do not certify global nonoverlap, hull coverage or planar embedding of an arbitrary supplied mesh. minimum_angle_degrees is a scaled floating diagnostic, NaN for no faces and potentially zero after extreme scale loss; it never controls topology. Empty-circle signs are exact for stored binary64.
%     datamunge.Mesh2D  A 2D triangular finite-element mesh: shared vertex coordinates plus a triangle connectivity list. Reuses datamunge::geometry's Point2D and (Delaunay-oriented, always-counterclockwise) Triangle index type directly rather than duplicating them. `nodes`/`triangles` are public fields for direct C++ construction, but -- same rationale as Mesh1D::nodes -- are not exposed to language bindings; node_at()/ triangle_at()/num_nodes()/num_triangles() are the cross-language-safe accessors.
%     datamunge.FEM2DResult  The nodal solution of a 2D FEM Poisson solve.
%     datamunge.FEM2D  Galerkin finite-element solver for the 2D Poisson equation -k * (u_xx + u_yy) = f(x, y) on a triangular mesh, using piecewise-linear (P1) Lagrange elements and Dirichlet boundary conditions only (this "basic" 2D solver does not offer FEM1D's Neumann/Robin options). Internally assembles a sparse stiffness system and solves it with the conjugate-gradient solver (datamunge::linalg::cg) -- the assembled matrices themselves are never exposed, matching this codebase's rule that linalg's sparse/dense matrix types are never SWIG-bound.
%     datamunge.AlphaBetaState  The position/velocity estimate returned by AlphaBetaFilter at each step.
%     datamunge.AlphaBetaFilter  The constant-gain alpha-beta ("g-h") filter: a constant-velocity tracker that uses the SAME predict/correct structure as KalmanFilter, but with fixed gains alpha (position correction) and beta (velocity correction) instead of a covariance-driven Kalman gain -- it is exactly the steady-state behavior a 1D constant-velocity KalmanFilter converges to once its covariance stops changing, with the covariance bookkeeping stripped out entirely. Far cheaper per step (no matrices at all) at the cost of not adapting its gain to changing noise conditions the way a real Kalman filter does.
%     datamunge.AlphaBetaGammaState  The position/velocity/acceleration estimate returned by AlphaBetaGammaFilter.
%     datamunge.AlphaBetaGammaFilter  The constant-gain alpha-beta-gamma filter: AlphaBetaFilter's constant-ACCELERATION generalization (a 3-state g-h-k filter), adding a third fixed gain gamma for the acceleration correction. Same fixed-gain-vs-adaptive-Kalman-gain tradeoff as AlphaBetaFilter, one derivative order higher.
%     datamunge.KalmanState  One state estimate + its covariance -- the common return shape for every "give me the state at every timestep" result in this module (filtered sequences, smoothed sequences, ensemble-filter output, ...).
%     datamunge.SmoothResult  The result of KalmanFilter::smooth(): the forward-pass (filtered, causal) estimates alongside the RTS backward-pass (smoothed, uses the whole sequence) estimates. smoothed.back() always exactly equals filtered.back() (the smoother has no future data at the final step); every earlier smoothed covariance is <= the corresponding filtered covariance (in the positive-semidefinite order) since it incorporates more information.
%     datamunge.KalmanFilter  The standard discrete-time linear Kalman filter: x_k = F x_{k-1} + w_k, z_k = H x_k + v_k, w_k ~ N(0, Q), v_k ~ N(0, R) -- the minimum-mean-squared-error linear   estimator for exactly this model. Stateful: holds the current (x, P) estimate,   mutated in place by predict()/update(); F, H, Q, R are fixed for the filter's   lifetime (construct a new filter if they change over time).
%     datamunge.VectorFunction  A vector-valued function R^n -> R^m: the state-transition or observation model supplied to this module's nonlinear filters (ExtendedKalmanFilter, UnscentedKalmanFilter, EnsembleKalmanFilter, ParticleFilter). Director-enabled (subclass directly in C++, or via SWIG directors in any bound language) so this module never needs to know the concrete form of the caller's dynamics.
%     datamunge.EnsembleKalmanFilter  The (stochastic, "perturbed observations") Ensemble Kalman Filter: represents the state distribution as a finite ensemble of sample state vectors rather than an explicit mean/covariance, propagating each member through the true nonlinear f (plus sampled process noise) and estimating the covariances the Kalman update needs from the ensemble's own sample statistics. Like UnscentedKalmanFilter, needs no Jacobian; unlike it, scales to very high-dimensional states (the classical use case this filter was designed for -- e.g. geophysical data assimilation with millions of state variables) since its cost scales with ensemble size, not state dimension squared/cubed. Statistical, not exact: converges toward the optimal (linear-Gaussian case: KalmanFilter's) estimate as `ensemble_size` grows, with Monte Carlo sampling noise at any finite size.
%     datamunge.ExtendedKalmanFilter  The Extended Kalman Filter: the standard first-order generalization of KalmanFilter to a nonlinear model x_k = f(x_{k-1}) + w_k, z_k = h(x_k) + v_k -- linearizes f and h (via numerical Jacobians, see detail::numerical_jacobian()) about the current state estimate at every step and otherwise runs the exact same predict/update algebra as the linear filter. Exactly reproduces KalmanFilter's result when `f` and `h` happen to be linear (their Jacobian is then the same constant matrix everywhere).
%     datamunge.InformationFilter  The Information Filter: the algebraic dual of KalmanFilter, tracking the information matrix Y = P^-1 and information vector y = Y x instead of (x, P) directly. Its measurement update is purely ADDITIVE (Y += H^T R^-1 H, y += H^T R^-1 z) -- the classical advantage of this form is that fusing several independent sensors, or distributing a filter across nodes that each see part of the measurement stream, is just summing information contributions, with no matrix inversion per sensor. The time-update (predict) step has no equally simple additive form for general F, so this implementation converts to covariance form, applies the ordinary KalmanFilter-style predict, and converts back -- mathematically exact, and produces IDENTICAL results to KalmanFilter given the same model (the two are the same estimator in a different parameterization, not an approximation of each other).
%     datamunge.ParticleFilter  A bootstrap particle filter (Sequential Importance Resampling): the fully general, non-Gaussian generalization of the Kalman family -- represents the state's entire posterior distribution as a weighted swarm of sample points rather than assuming any particular family of distribution (Gaussian, etc.), so it remains correct even for strongly non-Gaussian or multimodal posteriors where every other filter in this module (which all assume approximately-Gaussian beliefs) can fail. Costs scale with particle count and can need many particles in high dimensions ("the curse of dimensionality" for particle filters) -- EnsembleKalmanFilter is usually preferred for high-dimensional problems where the Gaussian assumption is acceptable.
%     datamunge.UnscentedKalmanFilter  The Unscented Kalman Filter: propagates a small deterministic set of "sigma points" through the true nonlinear f/h (rather than linearizing f/h itself, as ExtendedKalmanFilter does), then reconstructs the predicted mean/covariance from the propagated points' weighted statistics. Captures nonlinearity to (at least) second order without ever needing a Jacobian -- generally more accurate than EKF for strongly nonlinear models, at the cost of 2n+1 function evaluations per step instead of one. Exactly reproduces KalmanFilter's result when `f` and `h` are linear.
%     datamunge.BoundingBox  C++ class datamunge::geometry::BoundingBox.
%     datamunge.ClosestPairResult  Owned closest-point coordinate copies and legacy finite-double distance. Original field order/types are retained. Equal measured distances keep the earliest original index pair in the scan. Default storage alone is not a valid search result. No input references or global mutable state are retained.
%     datamunge.ConvexHullOptions  Limits for complete input storage and actual optional hull trace events. Full finite validation precedes max_points preflight; max_events is an online count of actual test/pop/push records, ignored by untraced calls. Zero disables either cap. No partial result escapes a failed cap.
%     datamunge.HullIndexedPoint  Owned distinct coordinate with the earliest matching input identity. Exact equality coalesces duplicates and signed zeros; sorting ties by original index retains the earliest coordinate representation. Output owns the point.
%     datamunge.ConvexHullWork  Actual sorting, deduplication and two-scan work, with checked counters. Comparison counts depend on the standard-library sort and original order. For at least two distinct points each pass pushes each point once. Tests count exact orientation requests; every nonpositive test causes one pop. max_stack is the greatest length of either separate chain, not their sum.
%     datamunge.HullEvent  Actual scan event, referring to indices in the trace's sorted unique points. candidate is the pending point; point is the pushed/popped point, or candidate for a test. a,b,turn are meaningful only for test events and otherwise zero. stack_size is the actual size after the event (unchanged for tests). Together these compact edits describe every stack state without quadratic snapshots.
%     datamunge.ConvexHullAnalysis  Owned strict hull, earliest original identities and actual work without history. Vertices start at the lexicographic minimum and run counterclockwise, without repeating the first. Collinear edge points are omitted. Empty gives no points; one distinct point gives one; a collinear set gives its sorted two endpoints. Identity vector aligns one-for-one with hull vertices.
%     datamunge.ConvexHullTrace  Owned actual sorted identities, compact stack edits and completed scan chains. lower/upper contain indices into sorted and include their endpoints. Inputs with fewer than two distinct points take no scans and have empty chains/events. Trace capture performs no extra sorting or orientation replay. All storage is independent of the caller, result and other snapshots; O(n) extra space.
%     datamunge.Callback  Base class for user-defined callbacks invoked by datamunge functions.
%     datamunge.RGB  A plotting color: 8-bit red, green, and blue channels plus an alpha opacity, the C++ counterpart of R's `rgb(r, g, b, alpha, maxColorValue = 255)`.
%     datamunge.DataSeries  One drawn layer of a plot (points, a line, bars, a box, a polygon, a label, or segments), as stored by RPlot's layer verbs and read back through Plot::series().
%     datamunge.ABLine  A straight reference line across the whole plotting area, as drawn by R's `abline(a, b)`, `abline(h =)` and `abline(v =)`.
%     datamunge.LegendEntry  One manually specified legend row, as drawn by R's `legend()`.
%     datamunge.Plot  Renderer-facing state shared by every 2-D plot: canvas, labels, axes, and series.
%     datamunge.RPlot  A single R-base-graphics-style plot, spelled `dm::Plot` in user code.
%     datamunge.RLayout  Tiles independently built plots into one multi-panel figure, like R's `par(mfrow = c(rows, cols))` followed by one plot per panel.
%     datamunge.DTWResult  Owned unrestricted scalar DTW result, preserving the original three-field layout. Distance is the unnormalized sum of absolute differences along the returned inclusive path. Default construction is an uncomputed placeholder. Copies own independent buffers. Positive lengths fit int; steps advance a, b or both by one.
%     datamunge.DTWWork  Logical work of the shared DTW recurrence and actual traceback. These are counts, not time. Validation, allocation, copies, comparisons and rendering are excluded. Each positive cell reads three predecessor slots, including infinite boundary slots. Every returned pair counts one traceback step.
%     datamunge.DTWComputation  Owned ordinary result and logical work, without retained cost-table history. Scratch consists of two cost rows and one predecessor byte per positive cell. No input references survive return. Copies own independent result paths.
%     datamunge.DTWTrace  Owned input, actual accumulated costs and chosen predecessors from one DTW run. Positive cells are row-major at i*b.size()+j; sentinel border is implicit. Costs and predecessor codes are captured during the original recurrence, not reconstructed afterward. Infinite cells mark unrepresentable costs. Logical capture entries are a.size()+b.size()+2*a.size()*b.size(): one per input scalar, cost and direction. Result paths, rolling scratch rows, scalar metadata and allocator bytes are excluded. Copies are independent.
%     datamunge.KDTree2D  A 2D k-d tree for nearest-neighbor / range queries over a fixed point set, built once (via a median-splitting, alternating-axis partition) at construction -- doesn't support incremental insertion/removal after that.
%     datamunge.Circle  C++ class datamunge::geometry::Circle.
%     datamunge.DiameterResult  C++ class datamunge::geometry::DiameterResult.
%     datamunge.MinimumBoundingRectangle  C++ class datamunge::geometry::MinimumBoundingRectangle.
%     datamunge.VoronoiDiagram  C++ class datamunge::geometry::VoronoiDiagram.
%     datamunge.LinalgMatrix  Binding counterpart of Mat/SpMat with shared, immutable expression operands. Transpose, multiplication and inverse actions defer native computation until eval(), values() or text(). Handles keep temporary operands alive across garbage collection. Sparse products use CSR; solves use the canonical dense LU/QR implementation. Concurrent const evaluation is safe; no evaluation cache is mutated.
%     datamunge.RHS  Right-hand side of a first-order ODE system dy/dt = f(t, y): R x R^n -> R^n. Director-enabled only in the Python binding, matching datamunge::filter::VectorFunction (the closest existing precedent -- a vector<double>-returning pure-virtual director): every other SWIG backend either has no director support at all, or -- confirmed by a real compile failure while wiring this class up in R -- cannot generate a working director wrapper for a virtual method that returns std::vector<double>. Every other binding can only use ODESolver::solve_builtin()'s fixed set of named systems, since a live user-supplied RHS requires exactly that subclassing.
%     datamunge.ODEOptions  C++ class datamunge::ode::ODEOptions.
%     datamunge.ODESolution  One numerically integrated trajectory: t[i] paired with y[i] (the full state vector at that time), in increasing-time order, always including both endpoints. `t` and `y` are public fields for direct C++ use, but are not exposed to language bindings (a container-typed struct *field getter* -- even a plain vector<double>, not just a nested vector<vector<double>> -- hits a swig-jse R-backend codegen bug that a same-shaped plain *method* return does not) -- time_at()/state_at()/size() are the cross-language-safe way to read it.
%     datamunge.ODESolver  Numerically integrates dy/dt = f(t, y) forward from t_start to t_end (t_end must be greater than t_start; this project's "basic" ODE support does not handle backward integration) given an initial state y0, via one of: explicit Euler, the explicit midpoint rule (RK2), classical Runge-Kutta (RK4), adaptive Dormand-Prince Runge-Kutta 4(5) (RK45), explicit Adams-Bashforth (order 2-4), or Adams-Bashforth-Moulton predictor-corrector (order 2-4 -- the "implicit" linear multistep option; this project uses a PECE predictor-corrector rather than a full Newton-iteration implicit solve, avoiding the need for a Jacobian). Every multistep method self-starts using classical RK4 for its first few steps.
%     datamunge.ACOROptions  C++ class datamunge::optim::ACOROptions.
