%     datamunge.to_rgba  A new RGBA image with the same colors as `src` and every pixel's alpha set to `src's` existing alpha (255 for modes with no alpha channel).
%     datamunge.split_channels  Splits `src` into one Grayscale-mode Image per channel, in the source's own channel order (so RGB -> {R, G, B}, RGBA -> {R, G, B, A}, etc.).
%     datamunge.merge_channels  The inverse of split_channels(): combines Grayscale-mode `planes` (same width and height, one plane per output channel, in channel order) into a single image of the matching mode. Throws std::invalid_argument if the plane count isn't 1-4 or the planes' dimensions disagree.
%     datamunge.dog_blobs  Difference-of-Gaussians blob detection (the detector SIFT keypoints are built on, without the orientation/descriptor stages): builds `num_scales` + 1 Gaussian- blurred copies of `img` at geometrically-spaced sigma_i = `sigma0` * 2^(i / num_scales), subtracts consecutive pairs to get `num_scales` DoG images, then keeps every point that is a strict extremum (min or max) among its 26 neighbors in the resulting (x, y, scale) volume -- 8 in its own DoG layer plus 9 in each adjacent layer -- and whose |response| exceeds `threshold`. A single octave only (no image-halving between octaves like full SIFT) -- simpler, and sufficient for detecting blobs across a single order-of-magnitude size range.
%     datamunge.structure_tensor  The Gaussian-smoothed second-moment (structure) tensor at every pixel: Sobel gradients Ix/Iy, their per-pixel outer product Ix^2/Iy^2/IxIy, then smoothed with a Gaussian window of the given `sigma` -- the shared computation behind both Harris and Shi-Tomasi corner response, which differ only in how they combine these three fields into a single scalar score.
%     datamunge.extract_local_maxima  Keeps only the response-field entries that are a strict local maximum within a (2*radius+1)^2 window AND exceed `threshold`, as a sorted-by-descending-response Corner list -- the standard non-max-suppression + thresholding pass shared by every response-map-based corner detector.
%     datamunge.harris_corners  Harris corner detection: response = det(M) - k*trace(M)^2 of the structure tensor M at every pixel, thresholded at `threshold_ratio` * (the response map's own max, so callers don't have to guess an absolute scale) and non-max-suppressed within a (2*nms_radius+1)^2 window. `k` is the classical Harris free parameter (0.04-0.06).
%     datamunge.shi_tomasi_corners  Shi-Tomasi ("good features to track") corner detection: response = the structure tensor's SMALLER eigenvalue at every pixel (a corner needs strong gradient variation in BOTH directions, so the weaker direction is the limiting factor) -- otherwise identical thresholding/NMS pipeline to harris_corners().
%     datamunge.fast_corners  FAST corner detection: a pixel is a corner if `min_contiguous` (out of 16) pixels on the Bresenham circle of radius 3 around it are ALL brighter than center+ `threshold` or ALL darker than center- `threshold`. This is the direct O(16) full-circle test (no accelerated high-speed pre-rejection on pixels 1/5/9/13) -- simpler to verify correct, at the cost of the constant-factor speed FAST is normally chosen for. Response is the found arc's mean absolute deviation from the center intensity.
%     datamunge.line_endpoints  The two points where the infinite line `line` crosses the border of a `image_width` x `image_height` image, for drawing/visualization. Returns false (and leaves the output points unchanged) if the line doesn't intersect the image rectangle at all (only possible for a line entirely outside it).
%     datamunge.hough_lines  Standard Hough line transform: every pixel of `img` whose grayscale luma is >= `edge_threshold` votes, in a rho-theta accumulator (theta stepped every degree over [0, pi), rho stepped every pixel over the image's diagonal range), for every line that could pass through it. Returns every accumulator cell with at least `min_votes`, non-max-suppressed within a (2*nms_radius+1) rho-theta cell window so a single real line doesn't produce a cluster of near-duplicate near-identical results. Intended input is an edge map (e.g. image::sobel_edges()'s output), not a raw photo.
%     datamunge.hough_circles  Gradient-direction-guided Hough circle transform: for every edge pixel (Sobel gradient magnitude >= `gradient_threshold` on `img's` grayscale conversion), and for every candidate radius in [`min_radius`, `max_radius`], votes for the two candidate centers at that distance along the pixel's gradient line (handles both bright-disk-on-dark and dark-disk-on-bright edge polarity). Each radius gets an independent 2D (cx, cy) accumulator and non-max suppression pass -- simpler to reason about than a combined 3D (cx, cy, r) accumulator, at the cost of not suppressing near-duplicate detections across adjacent radii.
%     datamunge.in_structuring_element  C++ datamunge::cv::detail::in_structuring_element.
%     datamunge.is_foreground  C++ datamunge::cv::detail::is_foreground.
%     datamunge.erode  Binary erosion: a pixel stays foreground (luma >= 128, matching image::threshold()'s convention) only if EVERY pixel within the structuring element centered on it is also foreground -- shrinks foreground regions and removes thin protrusions. Returns a Grayscale image with 0/255 values. Pixels outside the image count as background.
%     datamunge.dilate  Binary dilation: a pixel becomes foreground if ANY pixel within the structuring element centered on it is foreground -- grows foreground regions and fills small gaps. Returns a Grayscale image with 0/255 values.
%     datamunge.morphological_open  Morphological opening (erode then dilate): removes small foreground specks and thin protrusions while leaving the size of larger regions roughly unchanged.
%     datamunge.morphological_close  Morphological closing (dilate then erode): fills small holes and gaps in foreground regions while leaving their overall size roughly unchanged.
%     datamunge.lucas_kanade_optical_flow  Sparse Lucas-Kanade optical flow: for each of `points`, solves the classical 2x2 windowed least-squares system (built from Sobel spatial gradients Ix/Iy on `prev` and the temporal gradient It = next - prev, both accumulated over a (2*window_radius+1)^2 window) for the local translation (dx, dy) that best explains the window's brightness change under the standard optical-flow brightness-constancy assumption. Single-level (not the pyramidal coarse-to-fine extension real implementations use for large motions) -- correctly handles motions up to roughly `window_radius` pixels, which is this module's documented scope tradeoff; use gaussian_pyramid() externally and call this once per level if larger motions matter.
%     datamunge.box_kernel  A normalized (sums to 1) box blur kernel of the given odd size.
%     datamunge.gaussian_kernel  A normalized 2D Gaussian kernel with standard deviation `sigma`; the kernel radius is chosen as ceil(3*sigma) (covers >99.7% of the distribution's mass), size = 2*radius+1.
%     datamunge.convolve  Applies a square convolution `kernel` to every channel of `src` (alpha included), clamping accesses at the border (edge-replicate padding, avoiding any darkening artifact a zero-padded border would introduce). `kernel's` side length must be odd.
%     datamunge.box_blur  C++ datamunge::image::box_blur.
%     datamunge.gaussian_blur  C++ datamunge::image::gaussian_blur.
%     datamunge.sharpen  Sharpens `src` via an unsharp-mask kernel: the center weight grows (and the neighbor weights, already negative, grow more negative) as `amount` increases past its neutral value of 0 (0 = no change).
%     datamunge.sobel_edges  Sobel gradient-magnitude edge detection: converts to grayscale first, then computes the horizontal and vertical Sobel gradients at every pixel and returns their Euclidean magnitude (clamped to [0, 255]) as a new Grayscale image.
%     datamunge.adjust_brightness  Adds `delta` to every color channel (alpha untouched), clamped to [0, 255]. Negative `delta` darkens, positive brightens.
%     datamunge.adjust_contrast  Scales every color channel's distance from mid-gray (128) by `factor` (alpha untouched), clamped to [0, 255]. factor=1 is a no-op; factor=0 collapses to solid gray; factor>1 increases contrast.
%     datamunge.threshold  A binary (black/white) Grayscale image: pixels whose luma (rgb_to_gray) is >= `level` become 255 (white), everything else becomes 0 (black).
%     datamunge.lerp_byte  C++ datamunge::image::detail::lerp_byte.
%     datamunge.bilinear_sample  C++ datamunge::image::detail::bilinear_sample.
%     datamunge.resize  A new image of size `new_width` x `new_height`, resampled from `src`. Nearest maps each destination pixel to its closest source pixel (blocky, but exact for integer scale factors); Bilinear interpolates the four surrounding source pixels (smoother, the usual default for photographic content).
%     datamunge.crop  The sub-image [x, x+width) x [y, y+height) of `src`. Throws std::out_of_range if the requested rectangle isn't fully contained in `src`.
%     datamunge.flip_horizontal  A new image with every row reversed left-to-right (mirror image).
%     datamunge.flip_vertical  A new image with every column reversed top-to-bottom (upside down).
%     datamunge.rotate90  A new image rotated clockwise by exactly 90, 180, or 270 degrees (any other value throws std::invalid_argument -- use rotate() for arbitrary angles). 90/270 swap width and height; 180 keeps them.
%     datamunge.rotate  A new image rotated clockwise by an arbitrary angle (in degrees) about its center, via inverse-mapping + bilinear resampling. The output canvas is exactly large enough to contain the fully-rotated source (like Pillow's expand=True), and pixels sourced from outside the original image are filled with `fill_color`.
%     datamunge.gaussian_pyramid  The classical Gaussian pyramid: level 0 is `img` itself; each subsequent level is the previous level Gaussian-blurred (sigma = `sigma`) then downsampled by 2x (bilinear resize) -- a coarse-to-fine multi-resolution representation used throughout this module (e.g. as the natural foundation for a pyramidal optical-flow extension, though lucas_kanade_optical_flow() itself stays single-level). Stops early (producing fewer than `levels` images) if a level's dimensions would shrink below 1x1. Throws std::invalid_argument for levels < 1.
%     datamunge.signed_difference_image  Per-channel signed difference (cur - upsampled), re-centered at 128 and clamped to [0, 255] so it stores in an ordinary Image -- the standard way to make a Laplacian pyramid level (which is naturally signed) visualizable/storable without introducing a separate signed-pixel image type just for this.
%     datamunge.laplacian_pyramid  The Laplacian pyramid: for every level except the last, level_i = (Gaussian level i) minus (Gaussian level i+1 upsampled back to level i's size) -- the detail lost by blurring+downsampling at that scale, re-centered at 128 (see detail::signed_difference_image()) so it stores as an ordinary image; the final level is the smallest Gaussian level kept as-is (the residual low-frequency image the whole pyramid was built from). Reconstructing the original from a Laplacian pyramid is the classical use (progressively add each un-recentered level back onto the upsampled reconstruction-so-far) but isn't implemented here -- this module only needed the decomposition direction.
%     datamunge.connected_components  Labels every foreground (luma >= 128) connected region of `img` with a distinct positive integer via flood fill (simple breadth-first flood fill per unvisited foreground pixel, rather than the classical two-pass union-find labeling algorithm -- same asymptotic complexity, much easier to verify correct, matching this module's established simplicity-over-cleverness bias). `eight_connected` selects 8- neighborhood (diagonals count) vs. 4-neighborhood (orthogonal only) connectivity.
%     datamunge.otsu_threshold  Otsu's method: the grayscale threshold (0-255) that maximizes the between-class variance of the pixels it would split into "below" and "at-or-above" groups -- the standard automatic threshold selection when a fixed threshold isn't known in advance. Pairs directly with image::threshold(img, otsu_threshold(img)).
%     datamunge.kmeans_segment  K-means color segmentation: clusters every pixel of `img` by its RGB value into `k` clusters (Lloyd's algorithm, deterministic fixed-seed initialization by sampling k distinct pixels) and returns a new RGB image where every pixel is replaced by its assigned cluster's mean color. Throws std::invalid_argument if `k` exceeds the number of distinct pixels available to seed from.
%     datamunge.data_home  The cache root where downloaded datasets are stored.
%     datamunge.downloadable  The names fetch() accepts, sorted: "california_housing", "cifar10", "cifar100", "diamonds", "fashion_mnist", "karate_club", "mnist", "rebot_devarm", "sudoku".
%     datamunge.fetch  Makes sure a downloadable dataset is on disk and returns where it is.
%     datamunge.download_file  Downloads `url` to `destination` with curl, unless the destination already exists.
%     datamunge.make_uniform_mesh1d  Builds a uniform mesh of `num_elements` equal-length elements on [a, b]. Throws std::invalid_argument if num_elements is 0 or b <= a.
%     datamunge.squared_distance  Evaluate squared Euclidean distance using the original double arithmetic. No finite/range validation. Differences, products and sum can overflow, underflow or lose low bits; NaNs propagate according to floating arithmetic.
%     datamunge.distance  Return sqrt(squared_distance(a,b)) with its original range limitations. A finite mathematical distance may overflow through the squared intermediate; this convenience primitive performs no checked hypot or input validation.
%     datamunge.cross  Evaluate the original double determinant (b-a) cross (c-a). A positive exact determinant means counterclockwise, but this approximation can cancel, overflow or underflow and its returned sign is not certified. No tolerance or finite validation is applied. orientation_sign() supplies an exact sign for finite stored binary64 points; this primitive remains unchanged.
%     datamunge.delaunay_triangulation  Construct a complete unconstrained Delaunay triangulation with exact signs. Retains the original one-vector signature and Triangle layout. Finite coordinate validation precedes even trivial results. Duplicates coalesce to earliest ids; collinear/fewer-than-three distinct sites return no faces. Uses Bowyer-Watson cavity replacement with symbolic hull ghosts, avoiding any finite super-triangle whose proximity could remove valid narrow faces. All orientation/incircle signs are exact for finite stored binary64, including subnormals/extreme scales. Cocircular ties retain existing faces under the ordering in DelaunayTrace. Faces are strictly CCW, contain no ghosts and reference original ids. Finite output order is canonical as documented on DelaunayTrace. No boundary constraints, holes, Steiner refinement or minimum-angle guarantee is supplied. Input unchanged; O(n log(n+1)+u^2 log(u+1)) worst-case work for n records/u distinct sites with ordered cavity maps and full live scans; O(n+u) working/output storage, no shared state or optional resource caps. Exact-predicate cost is bounded per binary64 query.
%     datamunge.delaunay_trace  Capture actual symbolic-boundary cavity transitions from the same solver. No replay. Full finite validation precedes the original-record cap and all allocation. max_tests bounds cumulative incircle plus hull-side calls, checked before each complete live-face scan. Trace entries charge one per insertion plus removed faces, boundary edges and added faces, including ghosts, checked before retaining the event. Points/maps/final output and temporary cavities are excluded from that entry count; caps do not bound bytes or wall time. Zero disables only an optional cap. O(sum cavity/event sizes) additional history, at worst O(u^2), beyond the ordinary solver. Failure returns no partial result.
%     datamunge.delaunay_analyze  Measure a supplied mesh and test every nonvertex site against every circle. Performs the structural checks on DelaunayAnalysis first. The complete prospective circle count faces*(unique_sites-3) is checked before circle queries; zero cap disables only that limit. O(n log(n+1)+f log(f+1)+fu) work and O(n+f) storage; minimum angles are floating diagnostics and are not certified bounds. This is a local property analysis, not a global embedding/coverage certificate.
%     datamunge.delaunay_uniform_cloud  Generate the report's seeded uniform square-cloud control in the core. Uses mt19937(seed) and uniform_real_distribution<double>(0,extent), x then y for each point. Same-library runs reproduce the original report's seed20250728 and 30-site square when defaults are used. Standard distribution algorithms need not match across C++ libraries, so the seed is not a universal byte fixture. Coincident draws are allowed and later triangulation deduplicates them. O(count) work/storage, owned output, no global RNG or shared mutation.
%     datamunge.make_rectangular_mesh2d  Builds a structured rectangular mesh on [x0, x1] x [y0, y1]: nx * ny grid cells, each split into two counterclockwise triangles, for a total of nx*ny*2 triangles and (nx+1)*(ny+1) nodes, ordered row-major (x fastest, then y). Throws std::invalid_argument if nx or ny is 0, or if x1 <= x0 or y1 <= y0.
%     datamunge.make_mesh2d_from_points  Builds a mesh over the convex hull of an arbitrary point cloud via Delaunay triangulation (datamunge::geometry::delaunay_triangulation) -- useful for unstructured domains a structured grid does not fit.
%     datamunge.boundary_nodes  Node indices lying on the mesh's outer boundary: every node incident to a boundary edge (an edge belonging to exactly one triangle). Works generically for both a structured rectangular mesh (the domain's four sides) and an unstructured Delaunay mesh (the convex hull), returned in ascending order with no duplicates.
%     datamunge.bounding_box  The axis-aligned bounding box of `points`. Throws std::invalid_argument for an empty point set.
%     datamunge.closest_pair  Return the closest point copies and finite-double distance using the checked all-pairs kernel. Validates at least two finite points, visits every original i<j pair and compares widened hypot distances. Equal rounded distances keep the lexicographically first index pair. Duplicates are valid distinct records and can yield zero. No early stop, sorting or spatial pruning; O(n^2) work and O(1) auxiliary storage. No input mutation/shared state. Widened comparison can change former false zero/infinite results and last-bit ties; it is not an exact-real predicate. The old call/result layout remains; indexed/traced variants retain input ids.
%     datamunge.convex_hull  Compute Andrew's strict monotone-chain hull using exact binary64 orientation signs. Validates all coordinates before internal indexed copies or sorting; the original by-value argument copy occurs at the call boundary. Exact coordinate equality removes duplicates (including signed zero); the earliest matching original record supplies its representation. Returns lexicographic-minimum first, counterclockwise, no repeated endpoint and no collinear edge interiors. Empty/singleton/two-point/collinear outputs follow ConvexHullAnalysis. O(n log n) comparisons plus O(n) bounded exact-sign tests, O(n) scratch/output, no global state. The legacy by-value signature is retained and has no optional caps. Other geometry routines that use cross() retain their own floating contracts.
%     datamunge.convex_hull_analyze  Execute the same exact-sign hull kernel with identities and actual work. Same ordering, degeneracy and complexity contract as convex_hull(). Full finite validation precedes the complete max_points cap and scratch allocation. No events or completed-chain snapshots are retained; event cap is ignored.
%     datamunge.convex_hull_trace  Capture the hull kernel's actual stack events without replay or quadratic snapshots. Same computation/work as analyze, plus O(n) owned sorted records, compact test/pop/push edits and completed lower/upper chains. Positive max_events is checked online on actual records, including pushes; zero disables the cap.
%     datamunge.sample_gaussian_points  Draw unsnapped independent standard-normal x,y pairs with a local 32-bit MT stream. Reproduces the original hull report's mt19937/normal_distribution call order, including its cached normal variate. Distribution mappings can differ between standard libraries; a seed alone does not promise portable coordinate bits. Complete count cap precedes allocation. Empty count is valid. O(count) expected work/storage; no global RNG or mutation. Finite draws are checked before return.
%     datamunge.terrain_colors  `n` colours from green through yellow and brown to near-white, as R's `terrain.colors(n)`.
%     datamunge.diverging_colors  `n` colours from dark blue through white to dark red, a diverging palette for signed values such as correlations (ColorBrewer's RdBu, reversed so that low is blue).
%     datamunge.viridis_colors  `n` colours of the viridis scale, from dark purple through blue and green to yellow, matching R's `viridisLite::viridis(n)` at n = 10.
%     datamunge.value_colors  One colour per value on a continuous scale, for colouring scatter points by a numeric variable (ggplot2's `scale_colour_viridis_c()`).
%     datamunge.dtw_trace_entries  Compute the complete logical capture size without allocating a DTW matrix. Checks positive lengths, int path-index representability and size_t geometry. A representable count does not promise that the corresponding buffers fit memory.
%     datamunge.dynamic_time_warping_work  Compute unrestricted absolute-loss DTW and actual logical work without cost history. Owns the result; borrows finite inputs without mutation. No band, slope bound, step weighting, length normalization or fitted parameters. Finite ties choose diagonal, then advance_a, then advance_b. O(n*m) recurrence time, O(n*m+n+m) scratch/output, with only two double cost rows and n*m predecessor bytes. Complete input/geometry validation precedes allocation. Positive overflowing off-path costs remain infinity; only a nonfinite final optimum is rejected. Under ordinary double arithmetic the returned witness sums to the returned distance in forward order. Concurrent independent calls share no mutable state.
%     datamunge.dynamic_time_warping  Preserve the original two-argument unrestricted absolute-loss DTW entry point. Same validation, ownership, deterministic ties, numerical limits and complexity as dynamic_time_warping_work(); discards only its work counters. This distance is not a metric: unequal stretched sequences can have zero cost and the triangle inequality can fail. Equal-length diagonal alignment bounds it by rigid L1 loss.
%     datamunge.dynamic_time_warping_trace  Capture actual positive-cell costs and predecessor choices from the shared DTW kernel. Validates complete finite inputs/geometry before checking the optional complete capture budget, then copies inputs and runs once. Owns every snapshot. No partial trace/result escapes on failure. Same variant/ties/numerical rules as ordinary DTW.
%     datamunge.dtw_path_cost  Validate a complete monotone witness and sum its unshifted absolute losses. Checks all finite input and path geometry before numerical accumulation: equal nonempty path lengths, in-bounds indices, exact endpoints and unit advances (1,0), (0,1), (1,1). This certifies feasibility/cost, not optimality. O(n+m+L) validation/sum, no allocations, input unchanged; no shared mutable state.
%     datamunge.plot_dtw_alignment  Draw two scalar series and their validated DTW correspondence segments natively. Costs use unshifted inputs; only drawing adds offset to a and subtracts it from b. Requires result.distance to equal the validated forward witness sum exactly; it does not independently prove optimality. Validates complete inputs/witness, finite plotted coordinates and optional path-pair cap before drawing allocation. Owns all series; no file I/O or model mutation. Concurrent calls share no state.
%     datamunge.discrete_frechet_distance  The discrete Fréchet distance between two point sequences ("curves") p and q (Eiter & Mannila, 1994): the minimum, over every monotone coupling between the two curves, of the MAXIMUM pairwise distance along that coupling -- informally, the shortest leash length needed for a person walking along p and a dog walking along q (both only moving forward, never backward) to stay connected the whole way. Computed via the standard O(n*m) dynamic program (bottom-up, not the naive exponential recursion). Differs from DTW in exactly one respect: DTW sums (cumulative cost) where Fréchet takes a max (bottleneck cost) -- Fréchet cares about the worst single moment of divergence, DTW about total accumulated divergence.
